 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a feed-forward method that predicts the geometric attributes (position, opacity, and covariance) of 3D Gaussians from a global volumetric feature representation, rather than optimizing them iteratively per scene [S2]. It solves the problem of slow convergence and ghosting artifacts inherent in per-pixel aligned feed-forward methods by accumulating depth information into a unified 3D volume [S2]. Unlike its siblings which often rely on local cost volumes or epipolar transformers, this approach predicts a global scene representation, improving consistency in urban-scale reconstruction [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a method for initializing and refining the geometric attributes (position, opacity, covariance) of 3D Gaussian primitives in a feed-forward manner, typically using a global volumetric representation rather than per-pixel alignment [S2]. It solves the problem of ghosting artifacts and multi-view inconsistencies inherent in existing feed-forward methods that predict per-pixel Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers [S2]. By accumulating depth information into a unified global volume and refining geometry in 3D space, it improves global consistency and reduces redundant primitives in overlapping regions [S2].
 
 ## How it works
